								<div class="title" id="post-title-3095926">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605970055" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1499288683 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Jul 2017 21:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3095926">
									

<p>I'll also add that I actually like things like <a href="/scp-1782">SCP-1782</a>, <a href="/scp-2782">SCP-2782</a>, <a href="/scp-3682">SCP-3682</a>, and their associated tales. Each stands on its own to an extent, but its only when they're taken as a whole that you can see the narrative of Ezekiel Iepureanu's attempts to force the hand of God.</p>
<p>There's a lot of narrative potential in telling a story in a fragmented, non-linear tangle of stories that each only give a partial view of the whole, and inviting others to build on that. Compare <em>Lost</em> and <em>Inception</em>, and the &quot;Thieves' World&quot; mythos.</p>
<p>Simple, straightforward storytelling isn't a bad thing, but neither is complex narrative.</p>

							</div>

								
